[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/ppp2pp1/3b1n1p/2p1p3/4P2B/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1_w_-_-_0_10"]

{ Plans of José Raúl Capablanca, world champion from 1921. First, the cut-off piece: a piece your pawns wall in, so it can win nothing and cannot come back to help. Watch his bishop on h4. }
10. Nd5 { Hastings, 1919, and you have Black against Winter, a British master. His knight jumps to d5, a mistake: the quiet pawn to h3 was better. Hit his bishop with your g pawn. [%csl Rh4][%cal Yg7g5] } 10... g5 { Your pawn on g5 blocks the pin on your knight and chases his bishop. If it steps back to g3, all it sees is your e pawn, and your bishop guards that. [%cal Gg5h4][%csl Ge5] } (10... Be7 { The bishop steps back to e7 and blocks the pin that way. His bishop on h4 keeps its free road. } 11. Nxe7+ Qxe7 12. h3 { About level, and his bishop on h4 still pins your knight to your queen. The g pawn push would have started a wall against it. [%cal Gg7g5][%csl Rh4] }) (10... c6 { The c pawn kicks his knight, but his bishop takes your knight on f6 first. } 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Ne3 { Better for him: the pawns in front of your king are split, and his knight on e3 eyes the hole on f5. [%csl Rf5] }) (10... Be6 { The bishop comes out to hit his knight, but his bishop gets to your knight first. } 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Nh4 { Better for him: your king has lost its g pawn, and his knight on h4 and his queen have an easy road to it. [%csl Rg8] }) (10... Nxd5 { Your knight was pinned to your queen: once it leaves f6 to take on d5, his bishop takes your queen. } 11. Bxd8 Nf4 12. Bh4 { Much better for him: his bishop took your queen and got safely back to h4. [%csl Rh4] }) 11. Nxf6+ { He takes your knight with check. Only your queen can take it back. } 11... Qxf6 { Your queen takes back, and your g5 pawn still attacks his bishop on h4: it has to move. [%cal Gg5h4] } 12. Bg3 { His bishop goes back to g3, where all it sees is your guarded pawn on e5. Now pin his knight with your bishop. [%cal Yc8g4,Rg3e5] } 12... Bg4 { Your bishop pins his knight to his queen. That knight is his only other minor piece, and it could come round to attack your pawn on e5. [%cal Gg4f3] } (12... Be6 { The bishop develops to e6 and leaves his knight on f3 alone. } 13. Nd2 a5 14. Nc4 { Level: his knight came round to c4, where it hits your bishop and your e pawn. [%cal Gc8g4][%csl Rc4] }) (12... a5 { The a pawn gains space on the queen side, and his knight on f3 is left alone. } 13. Nd2 a4 14. Nc4 { Level: his knight reached c4 and hits your e pawn, so his bishop on g3 still has a helper. [%cal Gc8g4] }) (12... b6 { The b pawn steps up, a quiet move, and his knight on f3 stays free. } 13. Nd2 Qg6 14. f3 { Level: his knight left and his f pawn stepped up, which opens a road back for his bishop through f2. [%cal Gc8g4] }) (12... Qg7 { Your queen steps to g7, next to your king, and his knight on f3 lives. } 13. Nd2 a5 14. a4 { Level: his knight is on its way round through d2, and his f pawn is free to step up and open a road back for his bishop. [%cal Gc8g4] }) (12... Kg7 { Your king steps up to g7, and his knight on f3 is left in peace. } 13. Nd2 a5 14. Nc4 { Level: his knight hopped to c4, and your plan against his bishop is on hold. [%cal Gc8g4] }) 13. h3 { He asks your bishop to decide. Take the knight. [%cal Yg4f3] } 13... Bxf3 { Your bishop takes his knight, the helper his bishop on g3 needed. } (13... Bh5 { The bishop keeps the pin from h5, and the trade on f3 waits a move. } 14. a4 Bxf3 15. Qxf3 Qxf3 16. gxf3 { About level, the same trade a move later: his knight is gone, and his bishop on g3 is hemmed in just the same. [%csl Rg3] }) (13... Be6 { The bishop steps back to e6, and his knight on f3 lives. } 14. a4 a5 15. b3 { Level: nothing of his is walled in yet, and his knight is free to join the fight. [%cal Gg4f3] }) (13... Bd7 { The bishop steps back to d7 and leaves his knight on f3 alone. } 14. a4 { Level: his knight stays, and your bishop sits quietly on d7. [%cal Gg4f3] }) (13... Bc8 { The bishop goes all the way back to c8, and his knight on f3 stays. } 14. Nh2 Qg6 15. Ng4 { Level: his knight came round to g4 through h2, and it hits your e pawn. [%cal Gg4f3] }) 14. Qxf3 { He takes back with the queen, and the queens face each other. Trade them. [%cal Yf6f3] } 14... Qxf3 { Trade queens. He must take back with his g pawn, and then his pawn on f2 can no longer step up to open a road back for his bishop. Now watch. [%tail] } (14... Qg6 { Your queen steps aside and keeps the queens on. } 15. h4 f6 16. h5 { Level: his queen stayed to help, and the pawns in front of his king are still whole. [%cal Gf6f3] }) (14... Qe6 { Your queen moves to e6 to avoid the trade. } 15. a4 Kg7 16. b3 { Level: the queens stay on, and his g pawn never has to take on f3. [%cal Gf6f3] }) 15. gxf3 f6 { Three of your pawns have walled in his bishop on g3: its only safe square is h2, and all it can hit is a pawn you guard twice. That is the cut-off piece. [%csl Ge5,Gf6,Gg5,Rg3] } 0-1


