{ The cut-off piece again. You have Black, a pawn up, and his king has castled on the queen side. }
10. Bf4 { Berlin, 1913, against Mieses, a veteran German master. His bishop to f4 eyes your pawn on c7, a fair move. You need not guard that pawn: what is your most active move? } 10... Qf6 { Your queen comes to life, hitting his bishop and his pawn on b2. And if his bishop takes on c7, it walks into a trap. [%cal Gf6f4,Gf6b2] } (10... d6 { The pawn to d6 shuts off his bishop's view of c7, a safe and quiet move. } 11. Bd3 Re6 12. Nf3 { Level: his bishop on d3 chased your rook, and he develops with gain of time. Your queen never got going. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... d5 { The pawn to d5 guards your rook, but his bishop can still chase it from d3. } 11. Bd3 Rxf4 12. Qxf4 { Level: you chose to give your rook for his bishop, and his queen on f4 stands well. Your queen never got going. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... Rd4 { The rook to d4 challenges his rook on the d column. } 11. Bd3 Bd6 12. Bxd6 Rxd6 { Level: the dark bishops came off, and your queen never got into the game. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... Rxf4 { Your rook takes his bishop, and his queen takes back: you give up the exchange. } 11. Qxf4 d5 12. Nf3 { Level: you are the exchange down for a pawn, and the game is balanced. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... Bd6 { Your bishop offers a trade on d6, and his bishop takes it. } 11. Bxd6 cxd6 12. Ne2 { Slightly better for him: your d pawns are doubled, and his knight comes out. [%csl Rd6] }) (10... h6 { The pawn to h6 gives your king air, but it leaves the pawn on c7 to his bishop. } 11. Bd3 Re6 12. Bxc7 Qe7 13. Bf4 { Level: his bishop took your c pawn and walked back to f4, and your extra pawn is gone. [%csl Rf4] }) (10... Bc5 { Your bishop swings to c5 and eyes his f2 pawn. } 11. Bd3 Ra4 12. Ne2 { About level: his bishop on d3 chased your rook to a4, and his pieces come out. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... Bf8 { The bishop goes home to f8, and the pawn on c7 is left to his bishop. } 11. Bxc7 Qf6 12. Ne2 { Level: your queen came to f6 a move late. His bishop took your c pawn, and it can walk home again. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... Re8 { The rook steps back to e8 and leaves the c7 pawn to his bishop. } 11. Bxc7 Qe7 12. a3 { Level: your extra pawn is gone, and nothing blocks his bishop's way back. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... Re6 { The rook swings to e6, heading for the g column. } 11. Nf3 Rg6 12. Ng5 { About level: your rook reached g6, but his knight on g5 blocks it, and your queen never came out. [%cal Gd8f6] }) (10... Re7 { The rook steps back to e7, a careful move, but your queen stays at home and his bishop gets time to reach g5. } 11. a3 Ba5 12. Bg5 { Slightly better for him: his bishop on g5 pins your rook to your queen. [%cal Rg5d8] }) (10... Re1 { The rook offers a trade on e1 at once. } 11. Rxe1 Bxe1 12. c3 { Better for him: now your bishop on e1 is the one cut off, with no safe square at all. [%csl Re1] }) (10... Bd2+ { A check with the bishop, but his bishop on f4 takes it. } 11. Bxd2 { Much better for him: his bishop took yours on d2, and you have only a pawn for it. [%csl Rd2] }) 11. Bxc7 { Suppose he grabs the pawn on c7. That is the mistake: the knight to h3 was the move. Can you make him pay? } 11... d6 { Pawn to d6, and his bishop on c7 is cut off: it has five squares to go to, and your pieces cover every one. [%csl Rc7][%cal Ga8b8,Gf6d8,Ga7b6,Gb4a5,Gb4d6] } (11... Qh6+ { The check drives his king to b1, and your queen leaves the fight. } 12. Kb1 d6 13. a3 Bc5 14. Ne2 { Slightly better for you, but much less than before: your queen is far away on h6, and his king stepped to safety. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... d5 { The pawn to d5 guards your rook, but it leaves the d6 square open. } 12. Bd3 Re7 13. Bf4 { About level: his bishop ran back to f4 along the open road, and it is free again. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... Bc5 { Your bishop swings to c5, but your d pawn stays at home. } 12. Bd3 Ra4 13. Re1 Kf8 { Level: the road back for his bishop on c7 stays open, and your extra pawn is gone. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... Re1 { Your rook dives to e1 to trade a pair of rooks. } 12. Rxe1 Bxe1 13. Nf3 Bb4 { Slightly better for him: a pair of rooks came off, your bishop had to run back, and his bishop on c7 gets time to escape. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... Bd2+ { A check with the bishop, but his rook simply takes it. } 12. Rxd2 { Much better for him: your bishop is gone for nothing. [%csl Rd2] }) (11... h6 { The pawn to h6 gives your king air, but the d6 square stays open for his bishop. } 12. Bd3 Re8 13. c3 { About level: his bishop on c7 kept its road home through d6, and the chance to shut it in is gone. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... Re7 { The rook steps back to e7, and his bishop walks home at once. } 12. Bf4 Re4 13. Nh3 { About level: his bishop went back to f4, the road your d pawn could have shut. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... Re6 { The rook steps back to e6, where his other bishop can hit it from c4. } 12. Bc4 Re7 13. Nf3 { Level: his bishop on c4 chased your rook, and the bishop on c7 still has its road home. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... Re8 { The rook goes back to e8, and your d pawn stays at home. } 12. a3 Bc5 13. Nf3 { About level: his a pawn chased your bishop, and his bishop on c7 is free to walk back. [%cal Gd7d6] }) (11... Bf8 { Your bishop goes home to f8, and his bishop on c7 gets a move to breathe. } 12. Ne2 d5 13. Nc3 { Level: his knight hits your rook on e4, and your d pawn went past d6: it can never shut in his bishop now. [%cal Gd7d6] }) 0-1


