{ Back to Hastings, 1919, eight moves after the wall went up. You have Black. }
23. Rha1 { His rooks double on the a column, a mistake: the pawn capture on b5 was better. His bishop on g3 is still walled in, far from the queen side. Where do you strike? [%csl Rg3][%cal Rg3e5] } 23... c4 { Your c pawn hits two of his pawns at once, on the wing his bishop cannot reach. [%cal Gc4b3,Gc4d3] } (23... Bc7 { The bishop steps back to guard your a pawn, and the queen side stays shut. } 24. Rd1 Rd8 25. c3 { Slightly better for you, but nothing opened on the queen side, where his walled-in bishop cannot help. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... b4 { The pawn to b4 locks the queen side, the one wing his bishop cannot reach. } 24. Rg1 Rd8 25. Raa1 { Level: with the queen side locked, his walled-in bishop no longer matters. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... bxa4 { You take on a4, and his rook takes back. } 24. Rxa4 Bc7 25. Rh1 { Level: his rook on a4 presses your pawn on a5, and nothing of yours broke through. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... Kd7 { The king heads for the queen side, but his pawn takes on b5 first. } 24. axb5 Rxb5 25. Kf1 { Level: the a column opened for his doubled rooks, and your a pawn is weak. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... Rd8 { The rook takes the d column, but his pawn takes on b5 first. } 24. axb5 cxb5 25. Rxa5 Rxa5 26. Rxa5 { Level, though you are a pawn down: a pair of rooks came off on a5, and your queen side play is over. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... Rb7 { The rook steps to b7, and his pawn takes on b5 first. } 24. axb5 Rxb5 25. Kf1 { Level: you took back on b5 with the rook, and nothing of his queen side broke. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... Rh8 { The rook takes the open h column, on the wrong wing. } 24. axb5 cxb5 25. Rxa5 Rxa5 26. Rxa5 { Level, though you are a pawn down: his rooks went to work on the a column while yours stood on h8. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... f5 { The f pawn hits his e pawn, on his bishop's wing. } 24. exf5+ Kxf5 { Level: your f pawn traded itself for his e pawn, and your wall around his bishop has one pawn fewer. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (23... g4 { The g pawn charges, and it leaves the wall around his bishop. } 24. axb5 gxf3+ 25. Kxf3 Rxb5 26. Ke3 { Slightly better for him: your g pawn is gone from g5, and his bishop on g3 is free. [%csl Rg3] }) (23... Rb6 { The rook steps up to b6, and his pawn takes on b5 first. } 24. axb5 cxb5 25. Rxa5 Rxa5 26. Rxa5 { Level, though you are a pawn down: the a column opened, his rook took your a pawn, and a pair of rooks came off. [%cal Gc5c4] }) 24. axb5 { He takes on b5 first. } 24... cxb3 { Your pawn takes on b3. His queen side pawns come apart, and your rook on b8 looks down the b column. [%cal Gb8b5] } (24... cxb5 { You take back on b5 with the c pawn, and your a pawn is left alone. } 25. Rxa5 cxb3 26. cxb3 Rxa5 27. Rxa5 { Level: a pair of rooks came off on the a column, and you are a pawn down. Taking on b3 at once kept the pressure. [%cal Gc4b3] }) (24... cxd3 { You take on d3 instead, and his pawn takes on c6. } 25. bxc6 d2 26. c7 { Slightly better for you, but his new pawn on c7 is dangerous, and his b3 pawn still guards his queen side. [%cal Gc4b3] }) (24... Rxb5 { Your rook takes on b5, but his d pawn takes your c pawn. } 25. dxc4 Rc5 26. Kf1 { Better for him: he won a pawn, and your queen side attack is gone. [%csl Rc4] }) (24... Bb4 { Your bishop steps to b4, and his pawn takes on c6. } 25. bxc6 cxb3 26. cxb3 { Level, though you are two pawns down: his pawns took on c6 and b3, and your attack fizzled. [%cal Gc4b3] }) (24... Rb6 { The rook steps to b6 to guard your c6 pawn. } 25. bxc4 cxb5 26. cxb5 Rxb5 27. c3 { Level, though you are a pawn down: his pawn took on c4, and the queen side opened on his terms. [%cal Gc4b3] }) (24... a4 { The a pawn hits his b3 pawn, and a chain of captures follows. } 25. bxa4 cxd3 26. cxd3 cxb5 27. axb5 Rxa2 28. Rxa2 Rxb5 { Level, though you are a pawn down: the trades emptied the queen side, and your pressure is gone. [%cal Gc4b3] }) (24... Rd8 { The rook takes the d column, but his pawn takes on c4. } 25. bxc4 cxb5 26. cxb5 { About level, though you are two pawns down: his pawns took on c4 and b5. [%cal Gc4b3] }) (24... Rb7 { The rook steps up to b7, and his pawns start taking. } 25. bxc6 Rba7 26. bxc4 { Better for him: his pawns took on c6 and c4, and you are three pawns down. [%cal Gc4b3] }) 25. cxb3 { He takes back with his c pawn. } 25... Rxb5 { Your rook takes back, and his lonely pawn on b3 is attacked. His bishop on g3 is walled in and cannot join the fight on this wing: you play where it cannot follow. [%csl Rb3,Rg3][%cal Gb5b3] } (25... cxb5 { The c pawn takes back, and your a pawn is left alone. } 26. Rxa5 Rxa5 27. Rxa5 { Level, though you are a pawn down: his rooks took your a pawn, and his bishop is still walled in. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (25... c5 { The pawn to c5 lets his rooks take your a pawn. } 26. Rxa5 Rxa5 27. Rxa5 { Level, though you are two pawns down: his rook took your a pawn, and his pawn on b5 blocks your rook on b8. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (25... Bb4 { The bishop comes to b4, and his b pawn takes on c6. } 26. bxc6 { Level, though you are two pawns down: his b pawn broke through to c6. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (25... Rb6 { The rook steps to b6, and his pawn takes on c6. } 26. bxc6 Rxb3 { Level: you took his b3 pawn, but his pawn on c6 is deep in your camp. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (25... Rb7 { The rook goes to b7, and his b pawn takes on c6. } 26. bxc6 Rxb3 { Level: you won the pawn on b3, but his passed pawn on c6 is a thorn. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (25... a4 { The a pawn hits his b3 pawn, and a pair of rooks comes off. } 26. Rxa4 Rxa4 27. bxa4 cxb5 28. axb5 Rxb5 { Level, though you are a pawn down: a pair of rooks came off, and your grip went with them. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (25... Rd8 { The rook goes to d8, and his d pawn pushes into your camp. } 26. d4 exd4 27. bxc6 { Level, though you are a pawn down: his pawn on c6 is passed, and your rook on d8 has little to do. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (25... Ra7 { The rook steps to a7 to guard your a pawn, and his b pawn takes on c6. } 26. bxc6 Rxb3 { Level, though you are a pawn down: you took his b3 pawn, but his pawn on c6 is passed and deep in your camp. [%cal Gb8b5] }) 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: the pawn storm, St Petersburg 1914"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/aJIm0h9j"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals (1921); Game 11, line 5449; from move 10"]
[FEN "2kr2nr/1pp1qppp/p1p5/4p3/4P1b1/2NPPN2/PPP3PP/R3QRK1 b - - 4 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
