{ The pawn storm: when the kings have castled on opposite wings, you march your own pawns at his king to open columns for your rooks, while your king stays safe. }
10... Nh6 { St Petersburg, 1914, and you have White against Janowski, famous for his bold attacks. His knight is slow on h6: his h pawn push was better. Put your rook behind your b pawn. [%cal Ya1b1][%csl Rc8] } 11. Rb1 { Your rook stands behind the b pawn. When the pawn marches at his king, the rook behind it guards every step. [%cal Gb1b7][%csl Rc8] } (11. a4 { The a pawn goes first, but no rook stands behind your pawns yet. } 11... f6 12. Na2 Bxf3 13. Rxf3 f5 { Level, and your storm is slow: the knight had to go to a2 to help, because no rook stood behind the pawns. [%cal Ga1b1] }) (11. Nh4 { The knight hops to h4, on the wrong wing for a storm. } 11... b6 12. Nf5 Bxf5 13. exf5 { Level: your knight traded itself for his bishop, and your queen side pawns never moved. [%cal Ga1b1] }) (11. b4 { The b pawn charges at once, but nothing guards it. } 11... Bxf3 12. Rxf3 Qxb4 { Slightly better for him: his queen took your b pawn, because no rook stood behind it. [%cal Ga1b1] }) (11. d4 { The pawn hits the centre instead, and the game opens up. } 11... Bxf3 12. Rxf3 exd4 13. exd4 Rxd4 { Better for him: he won your d pawn, and the storm never started. [%cal Ga1b1] }) (11. Ne2 { The knight heads for the king side, with no rook behind your pawns. } 11... f5 { Level: he struck back in the centre with his f pawn, and your storm has not started. [%cal Ga1b1] }) (11. Nb1 { The knight goes back to b1 to make room for the c pawn, a slow plan. } 11... f5 { Level: his f pawn hit your centre first, and your storm pawns are still at home. [%cal Ga1b1] }) (11. Na4 { The knight jumps to the rim on a4. } 11... Bxf3 12. Rxf3 b6 { Level: he traded his bishop for your other knight, and your knight on a4 now blocks your own a pawn. [%cal Ga1b1] }) 11... f6 { He guards his e pawn. Now push the b pawn: your rook guards it. [%cal Yb2b4] } 12. b4 { Your b pawn marches at his king, and his queen cannot take it: your rook guards it. [%cal Gb1b4][%csl Rc8] } (12. Ne2 { The knight heads for the king side, and your pawns wait. } 12... Be6 { Level: his bishop found a good square, and your storm has not moved. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (12. Nh4 { The knight jumps to h4, away from the storm. } 12... b6 { Level: he used the time to push his b pawn, and your storm is still at home. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (12. d4 { The centre push opens the game, and after the trades your d pawn falls. } 12... Bxf3 13. gxf3 exd4 14. exd4 Rxd4 { Better for him: he won the d pawn, and your storm is forgotten. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (12. a3 { The a pawn steps to a3 to prepare the b pawn push, a move slower. } 12... b6 13. b4 Kb7 { Level: the b pawn reached b4 a move late, and his king had time to step to b7. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (12. b3 { The b pawn takes one step, a slow start for the storm. } 12... Be6 13. b4 b6 { Level: your b pawn needed two moves to reach b4, and his bishop found e6 meanwhile. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (12. Qh4 { Your queen swings to h4, on the wrong wing for your storm. } 12... b6 13. h3 Bd7 { Level: your queen stares at the king side, but your queen side pawns have not moved. [%cal Gb2b4] }) 12... Nf7 { His knight reaches f7. Now bring your a pawn up next to the b pawn. [%cal Ya2a4] } 13. a4 { Your a and b pawns stand side by side, ready to hit the pawns in front of his king. [%cal Ga4b5,Gb4b5][%csl Rc8] } (13. b5 { The b pawn charges alone, and his a pawn takes it. } 13... axb5 14. h3 Bxf3 15. Rxf3 { Better for him: your b pawn is gone, because your a pawn was not there to take back. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (13. Nh4 { The knight hops to h4, and your a pawn stays at home. } 13... Nd6 { Level: his knight reached d6 and watches over his queen side. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (13. Ne2 { The knight reroutes, and your storm pauses. } 13... b6 { Level: your a pawn is still at home, so the push to b5 would just lose your b pawn. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (13. Rf2 { The rook steps up to guard your second row. } 13... b6 { Level: a solid move, but your a pawn is still at home. [%cal Ga2a4] }) 13... Bxf3 { He takes your knight, hoping trades will slow the storm. Take back with the rook, and keep your king's pawns whole. [%cal Yf1f3] } 14. Rxf3 { Your rook takes back, and the pawns in front of your own king stay whole. } (14. gxf3 { The pawn takes back, and your king is left with no pawn on the g column. } 14... Nd6 { Level: the pawns in front of your king are split, and he has a target for a storm of his own. [%cal Gf1f3] }) 14... b6 { He pushes his b pawn to stop yours, and that helps you: now no pawn of his guards the pawn on c6. His knight to d6 was the better move. Hit that pawn with your b pawn. [%cal Yb4b5][%csl Rc6] } 15. b5 { Your b pawn hits his pawn on c6, and only his pieces can guard it now. [%cal Gb5c6][%csl Rc6] } (15. a5 { The a pawn pushes on, but his b pawn steps past it. } 15... b5 16. Ne2 h5 { Level: his pawn on b5 locked the queen side, and your storm has nothing left to hit. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (15. Rf2 { The rook steps back to guard your second row, and the storm waits. } 15... Nd6 { About level: his knight reached d6, where it guards b5 against your pawn. [%cal Gb4b5] }) 15... cxb5 { He takes. Take back with your a pawn. [%cal Ya4b5] } 16. axb5 { Your a pawn takes back: a pawn has gone from in front of his king, and your pawn on b5 hits his a pawn. Now watch. [%tail] [%csl Gb5,Rc8][%cal Gb5a6] } (16. Nd5 { The knight jumps into d5 before you take back. } 16... Qc5 17. axb5 axb5 { About level: you are a pawn down, and his king still has a pawn in front of it on b5. [%cal Ga4b5] }) (16. Nxb5 { The knight takes on b5, but his a pawn takes the knight. } 16... axb5 { Much better for him: you lost a knight for a pawn. [%csl Rb5] }) (16. Rxb5 { Your rook takes on b5, but his a pawn takes the rook. } 16... axb5 { Much better for him: a whole rook for a pawn. [%csl Rb5] }) 16... axb5 17. Rxb5 { Your rook takes back on b5. His king on c8 has lost two pawns from its cover, and your rook now stands on the half-open b column: that is the pawn storm. [%cal Gb5b8][%csl Rc8] } 1-0


