{ The pawn storm again. You have Black, and the kings stand on opposite wings: his on b1, yours on h7. }
38. Rxd5 { Berlin, 1913, against Mieses, a veteran German master. He grabs your pawn on d5, a mistake: the pawn push to h4 was better. You are the exchange down, so make your pieces count at his king. } 38... Rb8 { Your rook takes the half-open b column and aims straight at his king on b1. [%cal Gb8b2][%csl Rb1] } (38... Re8 { The rook takes the open e column, far from his king. } 39. h4 Bxh4 40. Rd7 { Level: your rook is active, but his king on b1 has nothing coming at it. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Qc6 { Your queen steps to c6 and hits his rook on d5. } 39. g5 Bxg5 40. Qe4 { About level: he gave up his g pawn, his queen came to e4, and his king on b1 is left in peace. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Qxd1+ { Your queen takes his rook with check, and his other rook takes back. } 39. Rxd1 Rxd1+ 40. Kc2 { Slightly better for him: you traded your queen for his two rooks, and his queen is the strongest piece left. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Rc8 { The rook goes to c8 to guard your c pawn. } 39. h4 Bxh4 40. Rd7 { Slightly better for him: your rook guards the c pawn, but his king on b1 feels nothing. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Rxd5 { Your rook takes back on d5, the natural move, and his other rook takes back. } 39. Rxd5 Qb5 40. Rd2 { Much better for him: a pair of rooks came off on d5, you are still the exchange down, and no attack is left to make up for it. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Bxb2 { Your bishop grabs the pawn on b2, but that leaves your rook on d8 to his rook. } 39. Rxd8 { Much better for him: his rook took yours on d8. [%csl Rd8] }) 39. R1d2 { He brings his other rook to d2 to guard the pawn on b2. } 39... c4 { Your c pawn joins the storm and takes b3 and d3 from his rooks. Now watch. [%tail] [%cal Gc4b3,Gc4d3] } (39... Qxa3 { Your queen grabs the pawn on a3 right away. } 40. h4 Bxh4 41. Qh2 { Level: his queen came to life on h2, and one pawn was all you got. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (39... Bg5 { The bishop steps to g5, and the pressure on his king eases. } 40. Rc2 Qxa3 41. Qg3 Qxg3 42. hxg3 { About level: the queens came off, and your storm is over. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (39... Rb3 { The rook goes to b3 at once, but his rook can challenge it. } 40. R5d3 Qxa3 41. Rxb3 Qxb3 { About level: a pair of rooks came off, and his king is safe again. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (39... Bxb2 { Your bishop takes on b2, but his rook on d2 guards that pawn. } 40. Rxb2 { Much better for him: your bishop is gone for a pawn. [%csl Rb2] }) (39... Qb3 { Your queen goes to b3 and stares at his pawn on b2. } 40. R5d3 Qa4 41. Rh3 { About level: his rook came to d3 and chased your queen away, and your attack stalled. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (39... Re8 { The rook leaves the b column for e8. } 40. Re2 Rb8 { About level: your rook went to e8 and back to b8, and those two moves were lost. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (39... Rxb2+ { Your rook takes on b2 with check, a rook for a pawn. } 40. Rxb2 Qxa3 41. Qc2 Bxb2 { Much better for him: you won back the material, but his queen and rook now go after your king. [%csl Rh7] }) (39... Rb6 { The rook steps to b6, and his rook comes to d6 to trade it. } 40. Rd6 Qf4 41. Rxb6 axb6 { Better for him: a pair of rooks came off on b6, and your storm lost its rook. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (39... Rb7 { The rook steps up to b7, and his rook takes your c pawn. } 40. Rxc5 Rb6 { Better for him: he won your c pawn, and your rook only shuffles along the b column. [%cal Gc5c4] }) (39... Rb5 { The rook goes to b5, pointing at his pawn on b2. } 40. h4 Bxh4 41. f6 Bxf6 42. Qf3 { Better for him: you grabbed two pawns, but his queen is now close to your king, and your pieces stand far away on the queen side. [%cal Gc5c4] }) 40. g5 Bxg5 41. Rc2 Bf6 42. Rd6 Qxa3 43. Rxf6 gxf6 { You are two pawns up. Your rook on the b column pins his b pawn to his king, so it cannot take your queen on a3, and your c pawn is one step from joining the attack. [%csl Ga3,Rb1][%cal Gb8b1] } 0-1


