{ Back to St Petersburg, 1914, eight moves after your pawns tore open the cover of his king. You have White. }
24... Nf4 { His knight jumps in and hits yours on d5, a blunder: the knight to g5 was his better try. Must you save your knight? } 25. c5 { Your c pawn hits his queen on d6: the storm breaks through, and your knight on d5 can wait. [%cal Gc5d6] } (25. Nxf4 { Your knight takes his, and the lines at his king stay shut. } 25... Qxf4 26. Qf3 Qxf3 27. gxf3 { About level: the queens came off, and his king on b7 is safe again. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Qf3 { Your queen steps to f3, and his knight takes yours on d5. } 25... Nxd5 26. c5 Qe6 { Better for you, but a knight down: the c pawn push came one move late. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. e5 { The e pawn hits f6, but it opens nothing near his king. } 25... fxe5 26. Nxf4 exf4 { About level: the knights came off, and his king on b7 sits safely. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Nxb6 { Your knight grabs the pawn on b6, but his c pawn takes it. } 25... cxb6 { Slightly better for him: you gave a knight for a pawn. [%csl Rb6] }) (25. Qe1 { Your queen steps to e1, and his knight takes yours on d5. } 25... Nxd5 26. c5 Qe7 { Better for you, though a knight down: his knight got away with yours. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Qd2 { Your queen steps to d2, and his knight takes yours on d5. } 25... Nxd5 26. c5 Qe7 { Better for you, but you gave a knight: the storm still runs, only slower. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Rf1 { The rook lines up on the f column, and his knight takes yours on d5. } 25... Nxd5 26. cxd5 { Level: you took back with the c pawn, and the storm pawn that could break through is gone. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Ra1 { The rook goes to the a column, and his knight takes yours on d5. } 25... Nxd5 26. exd5 { Level: you took back with the e pawn, and his king on b7 has time to hide. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Rf2 { The rook steps to f2, and his knight takes yours on d5. } 25... Nxd5 26. exd5 { Level: the knight trade left his king in peace, and your chance for the c pawn push has passed. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Ra2 { The rook steps to a2, and his knight takes yours on d5. } 25... Nxd5 26. Qf3 Kb8 27. cxd5 { About level: your queen went to f3 first, and his king slipped away to b8. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (25. Nxc7 { Your knight takes on c7, but his queen takes it back. } 25... Qxc7 { Better for him: a knight for a pawn, and his king is still safe. [%csl Rc7] }) (25. Nxf6 { Your knight takes on f6, but his queen takes it back. } 25... Qxf6 { Much better for him: your knight went for one pawn, and his queen is now active. [%csl Rf6] }) 25... Nxd5 { He takes your knight, and his queen is still attacked. } 26. exd5 { Take back with the e pawn, and your c pawn still hits his queen. [%cal Gc5d6] } (26. cxd6 { Your pawn takes his queen, but his knight on d5 takes your queen on c3. } 26... Nxc3 27. Rxc3 cxd6 { Better for him: the queens are gone, and you are a pawn down in the ending. [%cal Ge4d5] }) (26. Qh3 { Your queen hits his rook on d7 instead of taking back. } 26... Nf4 { Better for you, but his knight escaped to f4, and you are a knight down. [%cal Ge4d5] }) (26. Qf3 { Your queen steps to f3 instead of taking back. } 26... Qe6 { Better for you, but a knight down: his queen stepped away from your c pawn, and his knight lives on d5. [%cal Ge4d5] }) (26. Qe1 { Your queen steps to e1 instead of taking back. } 26... Qe7 { Better for you, but a whole knight down: his queen ran to e7. [%cal Ge4d5] }) (26. Qd2 { Your queen steps to d2 instead of taking back. } 26... Qe7 { Better for you, but a knight down: the recapture on d5 was the move. [%cal Ge4d5] }) (26. c6+ { The pawn checks at once, but his knight on d5 is still alive. } 26... Kb8 27. exd5 { Much better for him: the check let his queen off the hook, and your storm is spent. [%cal Ge4d5] }) (26. cxb6 { Your pawn takes on b6, and his knight takes your queen on c3. } 26... Nxc3 27. Rxc3 cxb6 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen, and you are far behind in material. [%csl Rc3] }) 26... Qxd5 { His queen escapes by taking your pawn on d5. } 27. c6+ { Check from your c pawn, and it hits his rook on d7 at the same time. [%cal Gc6b7,Gc6d7] } (27. cxb6 { Your pawn takes on b6, and his c pawn takes back. } 27... cxb6 { Better for him: his king got a new pawn for cover, and your attack is spent. [%cal Gc5c6] }) (27. Qe3 { Your queen steps to e3, and the c pawn waits. } 27... Ka8 { Slightly better for him: his king hid in the corner, and your storm lost its moment. [%cal Gc5c6] }) (27. Re2 { The rook steps to e2, and the c pawn waits. } 27... Ka7 { Slightly better for him: his king stepped to a7, out of reach of your pawns. [%cal Gc5c6] }) (27. Qe1 { Your queen goes back to e1, and the c pawn waits. } 27... Ka7 { Slightly better for him: he had time to tuck his king away, and your pawn push lost its punch. [%cal Gc5c6] }) (27. Qa3 { Your queen steps to a3 and eyes his a pawn. } 27... Ka7 { Better for him: his king stepped out of your pawn's reach, and your storm stalled. [%cal Gc5c6] }) (27. Re1 { The rook takes the open e column, and your d pawn falls with check. } 27... Qxd4+ 28. Qxd4 Rxd4 29. cxb6 { Better for him: the queens came off, and you are a pawn down with your storm gone. [%cal Gc5c6] }) 27... Kb8 { His king steps to b8, and his rook on d7 is left to your pawn. } 28. cxd7 { Your pawn takes his rook. Now watch. [%tail] } 28... Qxd7 29. d5 { A rook for two pawns: your c pawn ran from c4 to d7 and paid for itself with his rook. That is the pawn storm, carried all the way to his king. [%csl Gd5] } 1-0


