{ Winning by indirect attack again. You have White. His king looks exposed, but your target is elsewhere: find the move that wins material. }
1. Nd4 { Your knight hits two targets: his bishop on c6, which nothing guards, and his pawn on f5, now hit twice and guarded once. [%cal Gd4c6,Gd4f5,Gd3f5,Rf8f5] } (1. b4 { The pawn hits c5, a fair try, but it wins nothing. } 1... Rad8 2. bxc5 bxc5 { Slightly better for you, but no pawn won: his pawn on c5 is back in place. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (1. Bc4 { The bishop pins his knight to his king, the obvious move. } 1... Rad8 2. Rd2 Kh8 { About level: his king stepped out of the pin, and no pawn fell. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (1. a3 { A quiet pawn move that wins nothing. } 1... Kh8 { About level: he used the time to tuck his king away. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (1. e4 { The pawn hits f5 and d5, but it opens a road for his knight. } 1... Nf4 2. Bc4+ Kh8 { Better for him: his knight on f4 hits your queen and the pawn on g2. [%csl Rf4] }) (1. Bxf5 { Your bishop takes on f5, but his rook takes it back. } 1... Rxf5 { Much better for him: you gave your bishop for a pawn. [%csl Rf5] }) (1. Rd2 { The rook steps up to d2, a quiet move. } 1... Kh8 { About level: his king stepped into the corner, and your pieces won nothing. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (1. Rc2 { The rook steps to c2, a quiet move. } 1... Kh8 { About level: a move each, and his loose bishop on c6 was never touched. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (1. Rc4 { The rook lifts to c4, and his knight jumps to b4. } 1... Nb4 { Level: his knight hits your bishop on d3, and the chance on c6 is gone. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (1. g4 { The g pawn hits his f pawn, but it loosens your own king. } 1... fxg4 { Better for him: he took your g pawn, and your king has lost a guard. [%csl Rg1] }) (1. Rxc5 { Your rook takes on c5, but his b pawn takes it back. } 1... bxc5 { Much better for him: you gave a rook for his c pawn. [%csl Rc5] }) (1. h3 { The pawn to h3 gives your king air, a quiet move. } 1... Kh8 { About level: his king stepped into the corner, and your knight never jumped to d4. [%cal Gf3d4] }) 1... cxd4 { His c pawn takes your knight. } 2. Rxc6 { Your rook takes his bishop on c6: the c column opened the moment his pawn left it. Now watch. [%tail] } (2. exd4 { Your pawn takes back on d4, and his bishop on c6 slips away. } 2... Qxe2 3. Bxe2 Be8 { Much better for him: the queens came off, and you are a piece down for a pawn. [%csl Re8] }) 2... Kh8 3. exd4 Qxe2 4. Bxe2 { You are a pawn up with the queens off. Your knight jump opened the c column onto his unguarded bishop, and then his d pawn had no guard: that is winning by indirect attack. [%csl Gd4] } 1-0


