{ Giving back material: when you are ahead and he is attacking, you return part of your extra material to kill his attack, and you stay ahead. You have White, the exchange up. }
25... Bb7 { Havana, 1913, against Janowski, one of the great attackers of the day. His bishop joins the attack, a mistake: the queen to f7 was better. Bring your last rook to the e column. [%cal Ya1e1][%csl Re5] } 26. Re1 { Your rook comes to the e column and hits his pawn on e5, the pawn that holds his attack together. [%cal Ge1e5][%csl Re5] } (26. f3 { The pawn to f3 shuts his bishop's long diagonal, but your rook stays idle on a1. [%cal Rb7f3] } 26... Re8 27. Rd3 e4 28. fxe4 Bxe4 { Level: his e pawn broke free, and his bishop now sits on e4 in the middle of the board. [%cal Ga1e1] }) (26. Rad1 { Your rook goes to d1, against his d pawn, which his e pawn guards. } 26... Qf7 27. Qxf7+ Kxf7 { Level: the queens came off, but his e pawn and his bishops still hold the middle. [%cal Ga1e1] }) (26. Qg4+ { A check on g4, and his king steps into the corner. } 26... Kh8 27. f3 Qf6 { About level: his queen on f6 now guards his e pawn, and your rook on a1 still has not moved. [%cal Ga1e1] }) 26... c5 { He props up his d pawn and keeps his attack going. His queen to g7 was more stubborn, and it would have left you a quieter game, still the exchange up. Double your rooks on the e column. [%cal Yd2e2] } 27. Rde2 { Your rooks double on the e column, and three of your pieces now aim at his pawn on e5, the heart of his attack. [%cal Ge2e5,Gh5e5][%csl Re5] } (27. f3 { The pawn to f3 shuts his bishop's long diagonal, but it gives him time to guard his e pawn. [%cal Rb7f3] } 27... Re8 28. Rde2 Qf7 29. Qxf7+ Kxf7 { Better for you, but slower: the queens came off, and you are still the exchange up, but his pawn on e5 stands firm. [%cal Gd2e2] }) (27. Qg5+ { A check on g5 offers the queen trade. } 27... Qg7 28. Qxg7+ Kxg7 { Slightly better for you: the queens came off, but his e pawn and his two bishops stay, and the extra exchange is hard to use. [%cal Gd2e2] }) (27. Qh3 { Your queen steps back to h3, and his pieces get time. } 27... Re8 28. f3 Bc7 { Slightly better for you: his rook came to e8 to guard his e pawn, and your queen stands idle on h3. [%cal Gd2e2] }) 27... Qe8 { His queen comes to e8 to guard e5, and it hits your queen. Now give the exchange back: your rook takes that pawn. [%cal Ye2e5] } 28. Rxe5 { You give the exchange back before his pieces break in: your rook takes the pawn on e5, and if his bishop takes it, your queen takes back. Now watch. [%tail] [%cal Ge2e5,Gh5e5] } (28. Qg5+ { A check on g5 first, and his queen steps in the way on g6. } 28... Qg6 29. Rxe5 Bxe5 30. Rxe5 Qxg5 31. Rxg5+ { Better for you: the same give-back one move later, and the queens came off just the same. [%cal Ge2e5] }) (28. Qh3 { Your queen steps back to h3 and keeps the exchange, but his attack goes on. } 28... f3 29. Qg3+ Qg6 30. gxf3 { Better for you, but the pawns in front of your king are broken, and his bishop on b7 bears down on your pawn on f3. [%cal Ge2e5] }) (28. Qxe8+ { You trade queens at once, and his e pawn stays. } 28... Rxe8 29. f3 Bc7 { Better for you: the queens are off, but his pawn on e5 and his two bishops stay, and you are still only the exchange up. [%cal Ge2e5] }) (28. Qg4+ { A check on g4 offers the queen trade. } 28... Qg6 29. Qxg6+ hxg6 { Slightly better for you: the queens came off, but his pawn on e5 still holds his bishops together. [%cal Ge2e5] }) 28... Bxe5 29. Qxe5 Qxe5 30. Rxe5 { The queens are off, his attack is gone, and you are a pawn up: you gave back the exchange for his bishop and his best pawn. That is giving back material. [%csl Ge5] } 1-0
